+---
+id: dl-008-array-holes-a-sparse-array-is-refused-not-closed-up-or-read-as-null
+type: decision-log
+title: "Array holes: a sparse array is refused, not closed up or read as null"
+status: pending
+---
+
+# Array holes: a sparse array is refused, not closed up or read as null
+
+## Context
+
+A JavaScript array can have holes — `[1, , 3]`, `const a = [1, 2]; a.length = 4`, `new Array(2)` —
+and a hole is not an element with a value, it is an index the array has no own property for. The JSON
+data model has no such thing: RFC 8259 arrays are sequences of values, and dl-004 made `cloneJson`
+the place where a value JavaScript admits and JSON does not is refused.
+
+`cloneJson` and `checkPatch` both walked their arrays with `Array.prototype.forEach` and
+`Array.prototype.map`, which **skip holes**. Nothing refused a hole, and nothing copied one either,
+so a hole silently disappeared:
+
+- `applyPatch({ a: [1, , 3] }, [])` returned `{ a: [1, 3] }` — the array one element shorter, and
+  every element after the hole answering to a different index, so `/a/1` in the same patch resolved
+  to `3`. The library's one promise, that the result is the document the patch describes, was broken
+  before the first operation ran.
+- a patch that was itself a sparse array — `[, { "op": "remove", "path": "/a" }]` — kept its hole
+  through `checkPatch`, and `applyPatch` then read `op` off `undefined`: a bare
+  `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined`, outside the `JsonPatchError` hierarchy the
+  README promises covers any failure of a patch.
+
+Three readings were available:
+
+- **close the hole up** — what the code did, by accident;
+- **read a hole as `null`**, which is what `JSON.stringify` does (`JSON.stringify([1, , 3])` is
+  `"[1,null,3]"`);
+- **refuse it**, as a value outside the JSON data model.
+
+## Decision
+
+A hole is refused. `cloneJson` walks an array by index and raises the `TypeError` of dl-004, naming
+the hole's own location — `the value at "/a/1" is a hole in a sparse array, which a JSON document
+cannot hold` — and `checkPatch` visits every index of the patch with `Array.from`, so a hole there is
+reported as `InvalidPatchError`: `operation 0 is not an object, but undefined`.
+
+**Refusing beats closing up**, which is data loss: the caller handed in an array of three elements
+and got two back, with no error, and a patch written against the indices they could see addressed
+other elements than they meant. There is no reading of RFC 6902 under which a patch may renumber a
+part of the document it does not touch.
+
+**Refusing beats `null`**, even though `JSON.stringify` chooses `null`, because a hole is the absence
+of a value and `null` is a value: a document round-tripped through this toolkit would gain members
+that a `test` could then find equal to `null`, and a caller who put a hole there by accident — a
+`length` assignment, an `Array(n)` never filled in — would never hear about it. dl-004 already
+refuses `[undefined]` rather than turning it into `[null]`; a hole is the same mistake written
+differently, and it gets the same answer.
+
+**It is the clone that refuses, not each operation.** dl-004 made `cloneJson` the data-model
+boundary, and the boundary is where this belongs: no array holes survive into a document or into an
+operation's `value`, so nothing downstream — the walk of a pointer, the equality of section 4.6 —
+has to consider them.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A document or a patch `value` holding a sparse array now raises instead of being silently rewritten.
+  A caller who was relying on the old behaviour was losing elements; the message names the index of
+  the hole, so the fix is to fill it in or to shorten the array.
+- A sparse patch array fails as `InvalidPatchError`, inside `JsonPatchError`, so `catch
+  (e) { if (e instanceof JsonPatchError) … }` is again enough to catch every failure of a patch.
+- `jsonEquals` compares with `Array.prototype.every`, which skips holes too, and would have found
+  `[1, , 3]` equal to `[1, 999, 3]`. It is unreachable now that no hole can enter a document or a
+  checked operation, and it stays unreachable as long as `cloneJson` remains the only way in — which
+  is the point of dl-004's boundary.
+- `cloneJson` costs one `Object.hasOwn` per array element. A dense array — every array that came out
+  of `JSON.parse` — pays that and nothing else.
