+
+# A test that cannot resolve its path is a PatchOperationError, not a TestFailedError
+
+## Context
+
+Callers report being surprised by a failed patch, and this is the case behind most of it. RFC 6902,
+section 4.6 says of `test` only that "the target location MUST be equal to the `value` value for the
+operation to be considered successful" — and, unlike sections 4.2 to 4.5, it never says the target
+location must exist. Read on its own, that sentence makes
+`{ "op": "test", "path": "/lock", "value": "mine" }` against a document with no `/lock` an
+unsuccessful test, which under dl-005 would be a `TestFailedError` — the precondition a caller
+retries on. The toolkit raises a plain `PatchOperationError` instead, which such a caller lets
+through to its alerting.
+
+Both classes terminate the patch, as section 5 requires, so this is not a question of whether the
+patch applies. It is a question of which `catch` a caller's retry sits in, and `TestFailedError`
+derives from `PatchOperationError`, so the two cannot both be right.
+
+## Decision
+
+The classification stands: a `test` whose `path` does not resolve raises `PatchOperationError`, and
+`TestFailedError` is kept for a location that is there and holds a different value. The README now
+says so, and says why, where the surprise is.
+
+**Section 4.1 settles it by exception.** It is `add`, and only `add`, that the RFC excuses from
+pointer resolution: "Although the pointer's error handling algorithm will thus be invoked, this
+specification defines the error handling behavior for `add` pointers to ignore that error and add the
+value as specified." Spelling that out for one operation says what the others do. RFC 6901, section 7
+makes a pointer that references a nonexistent value an error condition; for `test`, nothing overrides
+it, so a location the document has not got is a failure to resolve and not a comparison that came out
+false. There is no value there to be unequal to anything.
+
+**It is also the more useful of the two for the callers who meet it.** A `test` is written against a
+location the author believes exists; a `test` that cannot find it usually means the patch is pointed
+at the wrong document or the wrong version of one, which is the "the document is not what the patch
+assumed" case of dl-005, not the "retry" case. A caller who does want to treat absence as a failed
+precondition can write `{ "op": "test", "path": "/lock", "value": null }` only if absence and `null`
+are the same to them — they are not here, which is the whole reason dl-001 made `resolvePointer`
+throw instead of returning a fallback.
+
+**Changing it would be a breaking change for no gain**, and dl-005 says moving a condition between
+these classes is exactly that. A caller catching `TestFailedError` to retry would begin retrying
+against a document that cannot satisfy the patch at all.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The surprise is answered in the README rather than in the code: the `TestFailedError` entry now
+  states the boundary and cites section 4.1 for it, so a caller reading the error list is told before
+  they meet the error.
+- A caller who wants one `catch` for "the patch did not apply to this document" already has it:
+  `PatchOperationError` covers both, since `TestFailedError` derives from it. Only a caller who wants
+  to distinguish them has to care, and they are the caller this decision is for.
+- If a service ever genuinely needs "absent or different" as one condition, it belongs in a named
+  entry point or an operation of its own, not in a widened `TestFailedError`.
