@@ -0,0 +1,134 @@
+---
+id: task-003-review-how-patches-are-checked-and-applied-against-rfc-6902
+type: task
+title: "Review how patches are checked and applied against RFC 6902"
+status: pending
+---
+
+# Review how patches are checked and applied against RFC 6902
+
+## Request
+
+Users of `applyPatch` report surprising results: some patches they consider invalid are accepted,
+some edge cases give results they did not expect, and a patch that fails does not always behave the
+way they read the standard. Review how patches are checked and applied, and make the behaviour
+conform to RFC 6902 in every case, errors included.
+
+## What was done
+
+A review rather than a feature, so it started by establishing where the behaviour already conforms,
+to keep the changes to what is genuinely wrong.
+
+### What the review found conformant
+
+Measured, not assumed:
+
+- the 108 cases of the two community conformance suites for RFC 6902 (`json-patch/json-patch-tests`,
+  `tests.json` and `spec_tests.json`) pass, compared with object member order treated as
+  insignificant, as section 4.6 requires;
+- 300,000 differential cases against an independent implementation written straight from the RFC
+  text — random documents to depth 3 and patches of one or two operations, with paths drawn from the
+  document's own pointers and from a list of near-misses (`/-`, `/01`, `/-1`, past-the-end indices,
+  escapes) — diverge nowhere, neither in result nor in whether the patch failed;
+- 200,000 cases over the invalid-patch space (missing, mistyped and unknown `op`, `path`, `from` and
+  `value` members; values outside the data model) hold two invariants: `InvalidPatchError` is raised
+  for a patch independently of the document it is applied to, as dl-005 requires, and neither the
+  document nor the patch handed in is ever modified;
+- the normative requirements of sections 3 to 4.6 were walked one by one against `checkPatch`; all of
+  them were already enforced, including the three that implementations in the wild commonly relax —
+  no location created along the way, `add` bounded at one past the end of an array, and `-` refused
+  by every operation that needs its target to exist.
+
+A cross-check against the `rfc6902` package on npm diverged in 16 ways, every one of them that
+package being the lenient or incorrect side: it reads a non-`array-index` token on an array as index
+0, lets `add` write past the end of an array, and refuses `add`, `replace` and `copy` at the root of
+a document that is not an object. The toolkit is right in all 16.
+
+So the reported surprises are not, in the main, non-conformance. Two of the three are a genuine bug,
+and the third is a reading of section 4.6 that the RFC does not support; both are addressed below.
+
+### The bugs found and fixed
+
+**Array holes were silently swallowed, in the document and in the patch** (dl-008). `cloneJson` and
+`checkPatch` walked arrays with `forEach` and `map`, which skip holes. `applyPatch({ a: [1, , 3] },
+[])` returned `{ a: [1, 3] }`: an array one element shorter than the one handed in, with every index
+after the hole renumbered, so `/a/1` in the same patch addressed `3`. A patch that was itself sparse
+kept its hole through `checkPatch` and made `applyPatch` read `op` off `undefined` — a bare
+`TypeError`, outside the `JsonPatchError` hierarchy the README promises covers any failure of a
+patch. A hole is now refused as the value outside the JSON data model that it is: a `TypeError`
+naming the hole's location for a document, an `InvalidPatchError` for a patch's `value`, and
+`operation 0 is not an object, but undefined` for a hole in the patch array itself.
+
+**A location that does not exist read two different ways.** dl-005 has `unresolved()` in
+`src/json-patch.ts` phrase such a failure "as the pointer module phrases it, so that a failure reads
+the same whether the walk or the last reference token was what went wrong", and it did not: the walk
+produced `JSON Pointer "/a/b" does not resolve: …` and the last token produced `"/a" does not
+resolve: …`. The same condition now reads the same way wherever the walk stopped.
+
+### The surprise that was not a bug
+
+**A `test` whose `path` leads nowhere raises `PatchOperationError`, not `TestFailedError`** (dl-009).
+Section 4.6 never says the target of a `test` must exist, which read on its own makes a missing
+location an unsuccessful test; but section 4.1 excuses `add`, and only `add`, from the pointer error
+handling of RFC 6901, section 7, and that exception says what the other operations do. The
+classification stands — changing it would be the breaking change dl-005 warns about, and would send
+callers into a retry loop against a document that cannot satisfy the patch — and the README now
+states the boundary, with the citation, in the `TestFailedError` entry where callers meet it.
+
+### Code
+
+- `src/json-document.ts` — `cloneArray` walks every index and refuses a hole.
+- `src/json-patch-operation.ts` — `checkPatch` visits every index of the patch with `Array.from`.
+- `src/json-patch.ts` — `unresolved()` phrases a location that does not resolve as the pointer module
+  does.
+
+### Tests
+
+`test/json-patch.test.ts`, 182 cases, nine more than before, red before green:
+
+- a hole in the array of operations, written three ways (`[, op]`, `Array(2)`, an array extended past
+  its last element), is an `InvalidPatchError`;
+- a `value` holding a sparse array, directly or nested, is an `InvalidPatchError`;
+- a document holding a sparse array is a `TypeError`, the message names the hole's own location
+  (`/a/1`) and not the element after it, and the refusal happens rather than the indices after the
+  hole shifting under the rest of the patch;
+- a location that does not exist is phrased the same way whether the walk or the last reference token
+  was what failed;
+- five more pairs for the equality table of section 4.6 — an object or an array against a value of
+  another type — which covers the last reachable branch of `jsonEquals`.
+
+### Documentation
+
+`README.md`: the hole of a sparse array joins the list of values outside the JSON data model, with
+why it is refused rather than closed up or read as `null`; the `TestFailedError` entry states where
+its boundary with `PatchOperationError` runs and cites section 4.1 for it; the `InvalidPatchError`
+entry now also mentions the `from` of a `move` targeting the whole document, which dl-006 decided and
+the list had left out.
+
+## Decisions recorded
+
+- dl-008 — a sparse array is refused, not closed up and not read as `null`.
+- dl-009 — a `test` that cannot resolve its path stays a `PatchOperationError`.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `npm test` — 182 tests, 182 pass, 0 fail (139 for JSON Patch, 43 for JSON Pointer).
+- `npm run typecheck` — no errors, sources and tests.
+- `npm run build` — emits `dist/`.
+- `node --test --experimental-test-coverage` — 99.7% of lines, 99.0% of branches; the three
+  uncovered lines are the `throw cause` rethrows that guard against an error neither the pointer
+  module nor `cloneJson` can raise.
+- The conformance suites, the two fuzzers and the cross-check described above, re-run after the fix.
+
+## Follow-ups
+
+- The community conformance suites and the differential fuzzer live outside the repository, so
+  nothing guards against a regression in them. Vendoring `json-patch-tests` as a test fixture would —
+  it is small and declares itself public domain, but taking a third-party file into the repository is
+  a call for whoever owns the licensing, so it is left as a proposal.
+- `cloneJson` and `jsonEquals` recurse once per level of nesting, so a document a few thousand levels
+  deep raises `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded` rather than anything the error hierarchy
+  names. No configuration file or API payload is anything like that deep, and making either iterative
+  is a rewrite, not a fix; recorded here so the limit is known.
+- Still no linter, and `package.json` still declares no `main` or `exports`: both follow-ups of
+  task-001 and task-002 stand.
